From 2382289f7f803fded6106d88c1a948833f94ffba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Schachter <davidschachter@appdevforall.org>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 20:58:13 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] Limit the memory of the JavaScript runtime

The JavaScript runtime had no memory limit (the JS_SetMemoryLimit call
was commented out), so code that keeps allocating took all the memory of
the process. On a phone that ends with the operating system killing the
process, which in an app takes the app down with it.

Set a default limit of 64 MB per connection, which SQLITE_JS_DEFAULT_
MEMORY_LIMIT changes at build time, and add js_set_memory_limit(bytes)
to change it at run time; 0 means no limit, as in JS_SetMemoryLimit().
Code that needs more fails with an "out of memory" error, and the
connection stays usable. A QuickJS context takes about 54 KB, so the
default leaves room for over a thousand aggregate contexts at once.

QuickJS cannot always recover from running out of memory while it
creates a context: it can crash. So the limit is set once the
connection's context exists, and the context of each aggregate or
window function, and the trial one that js_create_aggregate() and
js_create_window() make, is created with the limit lifted; the limit
then applies again to the code that runs in it.

QuickJS collects cyclic garbage only when its memory use passes a
threshold, which it resets after each collection to 1.5 times the
memory still in use. With more than two thirds of the limit in use the
threshold was past the limit, so garbage filled the rest and code that
fits failed. The interrupt handler, js_set_memory_limit() and each
aggregate's setup now lower it, after a collection, to halfway between
the memory in use and the limit.

When the limit leaves no room even for the "out of memory" error,
QuickJS throws null, which reported "Unknown JavaScript exception". The
runtime now allocates through functions that count its memory as QuickJS
does, and a null exception after the memory in use reached the limit
reports "out of memory". JavaScript that catches it still receives null;
the README says so.

Add a Memory limit test: allocations under and over the default, code
that never stops allocating, raising, removing and lowering the limit,
and invalid arguments. It also creates and runs an aggregate under
small limits and with little memory left, which crashed while QuickJS
created its context under the limit. With 12 of 16 MB held, code that
makes many times the free memory in cyclic garbage runs, and so does an
aggregate over 300 groups; running out of memory through small
allocations reports "out of memory". Both fail without the collection
and reporting changes.

